Over the last months I ran some tests on the level compiler in
UnNetHack. The level compiler is different from the one in Vanilla.
It's an extended version of this patch http://bilious.alt.org/?20 by
Pasi Kallinen. Pasi added this to SporkHack and after I took it from
there and added it to UnNetHack, Pasi himself updated and extended it
for the UnNetHack code.

So I wanted to stress-test this level generation code to find bugs and
as a by-product of this process, generate some statistics on how
dungeons in UnNetHack look like. I added some hacks so that starting a
game would run automatically through the whole dungeon and output the
dungeon layout into a text file. Any error or warning from the code
would also generate a core dump and as the random seed got logged, I
could easily debug any issue that cropped up.

The code was simple enough to apply to Vanilla as well, so I ran some tests on
Vanilla as well.

The only bug I found with Vanilla was that if you get the Frontier
Town version of mine town, on the right side there might be not enough
space for downstairs placement. To my knowledge, this hasn't happened
ever to a player but it also is not a game breaker. You can always dig
down, use the upstairs to come back up. The game will place you at a
random position if there are no downstairs.

For both Vanilla and UnNetHack, I produced each 1 million dungeons.
The big room came up 40.04% in Vanilla and 40.07% in UnNetHack which
is close enough to the theoretical 40% that one would expect. Fort
Ludios was 74.80% in Vanilla which is slightly lower than the 74.9%
that have been calculated more than a decade ago by Robert R. Schneck.
https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/tzlaN5uzKw8

We don't have his code, so I can't reproduce his findings and I don't
know if the difference was caused by him not using 3.4.3 (although
unlikely, I think nothing changed in the level generation code), some
error in his or mine code changes, or maybe even just by using
different random number generators. The difference is also not that
big, so I don't think this is something to worry about.

In UnNetHack, Fort Ludios came up 93.51%, this is due to a change in
the portal placement code so that it would appear more often. The
nymph level was in 45% of all generated dungeons.

I made two charts that show the data for NetHack and UnNetHack.

I left out some levels because they are dependant on each other and
only clutter up the chart. For example, Sokoban is always directly
below Oracle, fakewiz1 and fakewiz2 have the same probabilities, or
the wizard's tower levels.

The charts:
http://bhaak.net/nethack/levels/vanilla.html
http://bhaak.net/nethack/levels/unnethack.html

I was surprised to see that the new UnNetHack levels don't influence
the placement of the Vanilla levels. Fort Ludios is the only exception
but we deliberately tweaked it so this was to expected.


The code for both Vanilla and UnNetHack are in branches in the normal
UnNetHack git repository:
